<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=roman-concrete">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Building and engineering</p><h1 class="enc-title">Roman concrete</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 150 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as opus caementicium</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Roman concrete, or opus caementicium, is a building material used in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>: a hydraulic-setting cement mixed with an aggregate, in widespread use from about 150 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> first used it in coastal underwater structures, probably in the harbours around Baiae before the end of the 2nd century BCE, and around 25 BCE Vitruvius recommended volcanic pozzolana for structural mortars. Its aggregates often included larger pieces than modern concrete, so it was laid rather than poured. The dome of the Pantheon, the world's largest and oldest unreinforced concrete dome, is built of it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roman-concrete.jpg" alt="Roman concrete" width="320" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: matankic, Original by Kleon3 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2015-Heerlen,_Thermenmuseum,_Romeins_beton_(retouched).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Many bridges, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqueduct">aqueducts</a> built with Roman concrete still stand. Some scholars believe it was developed a century before its widespread use from about 150 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> first used hydraulic concrete in coastal underwater structures, probably in the harbours around Baiae before the end of the 2nd century BCE. The harbour of Caesarea, built between 22 and 15 BCE, is a large-scale example, for which enormous quantities of pozzolana were imported from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pozzuoli">Puteoli</a>.</p><p>Vitruvius, writing around 25 BCE in his Ten Books on Architecture, recommended pozzolana, the volcanic sand of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pozzuoli">Pozzuoli</a>, for structural mortars, in a ratio of 1 part lime to 3 of pozzolana for buildings and 1 to 2 for underwater work. After the fire of 64 CE destroyed large parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a>'s new building code largely called for brick-faced concrete, which seems to have encouraged the brick and concrete industries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Like any concrete, it consists of an aggregate and a hydraulic mortar, a binder mixed with water that hardens over time. The aggregate varied and included rock, ceramic tile, lime clasts and brick rubble from demolished buildings; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> tuff was often used. Gypsum and quicklime served as binders, and volcanic dusts were favoured where they could be had. Roman concretes could usually set underwater, and they were laid rather than poured because their aggregates were often larger.</p><p>Builders also adapted it to an earthquake-prone land. Interruptions and internal constructions within walls and domes created discontinuities, so that parts of a building could shift slightly in a tremor. In the Pantheon the aggregate of the upper dome alternates layers of light tuff and pumice, giving a density of 1,350 kilograms per cubic metre, while the foundation uses travertine at 2,200. Interiors were finished with stucco, fresco or coloured marble.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>It was long thought that pozzolanic ash gave the concrete its strength. Research in 2023 found that lime clasts, once taken as a sign of poor technique, react with water seeping into cracks and let new calcium carbonate crystals reseal them, probably the result of mixing with quicklime while hot. Seawater reacting with volcanic ash and quicklime forms a rare crystal, tobermorite, which may resist fracturing. In 2013 the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, Berkeley described how a calcium-aluminium-silicate-hydrate compound binds the material. Since 2010 companies and municipalities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> have begun to explore Roman-style concrete using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> fly ash in place of volcanic ash.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Roman concrete” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The materials included wood, limestone, tuff, tiles, cement and mortar, with <b>Roman concrete</b> for foundations, corridor vaults and secondary walls.</blockquote><a href="/work/colosseum">Colosseum · How It Was Made</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 150 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Rome (first in the harbours near Baiae)</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Some scholars think it was developed a century earlier.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/reinforced-concrete">Reinforced concrete<span>1853</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=roman-concrete">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
